.TH "REPROCTL\-CLUSTER" "1" "2026" "reproctl" "Repro Development Tools"
.SH NAME
reproctl\-cluster \- manage the local kind cluster
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]reproctl cluster\f[R] \f[B]up\f[R] | \f[B]down\f[R]
[\f[B]\-\-force\f[R]] | \f[B]status\f[R] | \f[B]reset\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Manage the local kind Kubernetes cluster and its associated container
registry.
.SS Subcommands
.TP
\f[B]up\f[R]
Create the kind cluster and local container registry.
This operation is idempotent \(em if the cluster already exists, it is
left untouched.
.TP
\f[B]down\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-force\f[R]]
Tear down the cluster and registry.
If services are currently running in Tilt, a warning is displayed and
the operation is aborted unless \f[B]\-\-force\f[R] is specified.
.TP
\f[B]status\f[R]
Show the current state of the cluster and registry (running, stopped, or
not found).
With the global \f[B]\-\-json\f[R] flag, outputs a JSON object with
cluster running state and registry details.
.TP
\f[B]reset\f[R]
Equivalent to \f[B]down \-\-force\f[R] followed by \f[B]up\f[R].
Destroys the existing cluster and creates a fresh one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-\-force\f[R]
Used with \f[B]down\f[R].
Tears down the cluster even when services are still running in Tilt.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
reproctl cluster up
Create the cluster (no\-op if it already exists).
.TP
reproctl cluster status
Check whether the cluster is running.
.TP
reproctl \-\-json cluster status
Machine\-readable cluster and registry state.
.TP
reproctl cluster down \-\-force
Tear down even with running services.
.TP
reproctl cluster reset
Destroy and recreate the cluster.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[B]reproctl\f[R](1), \f[B]reproctl\-setup\f[R](1)
